course: money
lesson: 26
title: A bank account and a debit card
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bank account is a safe place for money: the bank keeps a careful record of how much is yours, and you can take it out or spend it when you need it.
explain: The bank does not keep your own tenner in a box with your name on it; it writes down "Josh: £10", and when Josh takes money out he gets ten pounds, just not the same note.
explain: A debit card spends money straight from your account, so it can only spend what is there: with £3 in the account, a £4.50 snack is declined at the till.
explain: Picture Ruby keeping her PIN on a sticky note next to her card: that is like taping your door key to the door, because anyone who finds both can empty the account.
explain: A banking app shows every payment in and out, and if a card is lost, one call to the bank freezes it so nobody else can use it.
fact: A bank account is a place at a bank where your money is kept and recorded; to deposit, or pay in, means to put money in.
fact: Zara's account had £0; after she pays in £20 of birthday money, it has £0 + £20 = £20.
fact: The balance is how much money is in your account; the bank records the amount, not the exact notes.
fact: A debit card uses your own money from your account, unlike a credit card, which borrows.
fact: With £15 in the account, a £20 game is declined, because the card cannot take you below £0.
fact: Insufficient funds means not enough money; with £3 in the account, a £4.50 snack is £4.50 − £3 = £1.50 short.
fact: A PIN, or personal identification number, is a secret 4-digit code for a card; never write it near the card or tell anyone except a parent.
fact: A transaction is a payment in or out; a statement or app lists them, like £2.50 + £4.99 + £1.20 = £8.69 spent in a week.
fact: If a card is lost, the bank can freeze or block it at once, so the money in the account stays safe.
fact: A transfer moves money between accounts; a standing order does it automatically, like £4 every Saturday, which is £16 over 4 Saturdays.
fact: Money in a bank is protected up to a large limit in many countries, even if the bank fails; money under a mattress can be stolen or burnt.
fact: It was reported that about 1 in 50 adults in the UK has no bank account; opening one usually needs ID and an address, which some people find hard to get.
exercise: What is a bank account? | A safe place at a bank where your money is kept and recorded | A box at home where you hide your coins | A shop where you can buy money cheaply | A card that lets you borrow as much as you like
exercise: Zara's account had £0. She pays in £20. What is her balance now? | £20 | £0 | £40 | £2
exercise: Josh puts his tenner in the bank. What does the bank keep? | A record that £10 is his | His exact note in a box with his name | Nothing at all, the money is gone | A photo of his note to prove it
exercise: What is a debit card? | A card that spends your own money from your account | A card that borrows money from the bank each time you pay | A card that gives you free money from the bank each month | A card that only works in cash machines when you are abroad
exercise: Mia has £15 in her account and tries to buy a £20 game with her debit card. What happens? | The card is declined, as she can't go below £0 | It works, and she owes the bank £5 later | It works, and the shop gives her £5 change from the till | The game is sold to her at £15 instead
exercise: Leo has £3 in his account and the snacks cost £4.50. How much is he short? | £1.50 | £7.50 | £2.50 | £1
exercise: What does "declined" mean when you pay by card? | The payment did not go through | The shop gave you a discount | The card paid twice by mistake | The bank sent you a reward
exercise: Ruby keeps her PIN on a sticky note in her purse. Why is that a bad idea? | Anyone who finds the card and the note can spend her money | The PIN stops working once it is written down | Sticky notes are not allowed to be kept in purses by law at all | The bank charges a fee for writing PINs down
exercise: What is a PIN? | A secret number that goes with your card | The long number printed on the front of the card | The name and address of your local bank branch | The total amount of money left in your account
exercise: Ben's app shows payments of £2.50, £4.99 and £1.20. How much did he spend? | £8.69 | £7.69 | £8.59 | £9.69
exercise: Ava loses her bank card. What should happen first? | Tell the bank, who can freeze it so nobody can use it | Wait a week to see if it turns up | Post the card number online to find it | Take all the money out quickly using another card from the bank
exercise: Kai gets £4 every Saturday by standing order. How much is that over 4 Saturdays? | £16 | £8 | £12 | £44
exercise: What is a standing order? | A regular automatic payment from one account to another | An order for food that is delivered to your door every week | A rule that you must stand in the queue at the bank to pay | A one-off payment you make at the till with your card in a shop
exercise: Ollie's gran keeps £500 under the mattress. What is the main risk? | It can be stolen or burnt, and then it is gone | It grows mouldy and somehow turns into £50 in the end | The bank will charge her a special fee for using a mattress | Nothing, because mattresses are the safest place for cash
exercise: Isla asks why some people have no bank account. What is a real reason? | Opening one usually needs ID and an address, which some find hard to get | Banks only let rich people open accounts | Bank accounts are banned for anyone over 60 | People with no account have too much money
exercise: About how many adults in the UK were reported to have no bank account? | About 1 in 50 | About 1 in 2 | About 1 in 5 | Nobody at all
test: Your mate goes, "If the bank's got my tenner, they must keep it in a drawer with my name on, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) What's true? | The bank records that £10 is yours; you get the same amount back, not the same note | He's right, every note has a drawer with a name | The bank spends it and you never get it back | The bank turns it into coins and posts them back
test: Leo's mate laughs, "Card declined, embarrassing, mate!" What actually happened? | There wasn't enough money in Leo's account for that payment | The shop doesn't like Leo, so it refused him | The card is broken and must be thrown away | The bank has closed Leo's account for ever
test: A Kiwi cousin says, "Heaps of my mates have debit cards, they're sweet as, you just tap." ("Heaps" means lots; "sweet as" means great.) What can a debit card NOT do? | Spend more money than is in the account | Pay for things in a shop by tapping | Show payments in a banking app | Spend money that is already in the account
test: What would happen if Ruby's purse, with her card and PIN note, was stolen? | The thief could take all £50 from her account | Nothing, a PIN only works for its owner's face | The bank would refuse to let anyone use it ever | The thief would have to pay Ruby £50
test: Ollie's gran says, "Banks? Back in my day we kept it under the mattress, safe as houses." What's the best reply? | Money in a bank is protected; under a mattress it can be stolen or burnt | She's right, mattresses are protected by the government | Banks lose everyone's money every year | Money under a mattress grows with interest
test: Kai says, "It's like magic, bro, £4 just turns up every Saturday!" What's really happening? | A standing order sends the money automatically from a parent's account | The bank prints £4 for every child each week | Kai's card makes new money if he waits a week | It's a mistake, and the bank will take it back
test: In a cartoon, a boy loses his card and says, "Oh well, it's just plastic." What should he really do? | Tell the bank quickly so they freeze it | Nothing, lost cards stop working by themselves | Make a new card out of cardboard | Wait until the next birthday for a new one
test: Ben's app shows: shop £2.50, game £4.99 — wait, it was £3.99 — shop £1.20. How much did he spend? | £7.69 | £8.69 | £6.69 | £7.79
test: Your Canadian cousin says, "Mom gave me twenty loonies for my account, eh." (A "loonie" is a Canadian one-dollar coin.) How much does she pay in? | 20 dollars | 1 dollar | 2 dollars | 200 dollars
test: Isla's friend says, "People without bank accounts are just lazy." What's a fairer answer? | Some find it hard to open one, for example without ID or an address | She's right, it only takes laziness to have none | Bank accounts are illegal for most families | Everyone in the UK has an account, so it's not true
test: Mia's mum says, "It only spends what's in there, no borrowing." Which card is she talking about? | A debit card | A credit card | A store loyalty card | A library card
exam: It is given that the balance of an account stands at £3, whereas a purchase of £4.50 is attempted by means of a debit card. Determine the shortfall. | £1.50 | £7.50 | £2.50 | £1
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a debit card? | An instrument that draws upon funds already held in the account of the holder | An instrument by which the holder borrows funds from the issuing bank | An instrument entitling the holder to an allowance from the government | An instrument usable exclusively in automated cash machines
exam: Ascertain the aggregate expenditure recorded in a statement comprising transactions of £2.50, £4.99 and £1.20. | £8.69 | £7.69 | £8.59 | £9.69
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with prudence upon the loss of a bank card. | Immediate notification of the bank, in order that the card be frozen | Deferral of any action whatsoever pending the possible recovery of the card | Publication of the details of the card in order to locate it | Withdrawal of the entire balance by means of a second card
